---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import NewsletterForm from "../../components/forms/newsletter-form.astro";
---
<Layout title="WebM Format Support" seo={{
  page_description: "Learn about WebM, the open-source video format built by Google. Ideal for transparent video (alpha channels) and highly efficient web delivery.",
  open_graph_type: "article"
}}>
  <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-12">
    <div class="text-sm text-secondary mb-4">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-primary">Home</a> &gt; <a href="/formats/" class="hover:text-primary">Formats</a> &gt; WebM
    </div>
    <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold mb-8">WebM</h1>
    <div class="prose max-w-none mb-12">
      <p class="lead text-xl text-secondary">
        WebM is an open, royalty-free <a href="/reference/containers">container format</a> created by Google specifically for HTML5 video on the web.
      </p>

      <h2>Under the Hood</h2>
      <p>
        Technically, WebM is a restricted subset of the <a href="/reference/mkv">Matroska (MKV)</a> container. While MKV can hold almost any codec, WebM restricts the internal streams to specific, royalty-free open codecs:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Video:</strong> VP8, <a href="/reference/vp9">VP9</a>, or <a href="/reference/av1">AV1</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>Audio:</strong> Vorbis or Opus.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>The WebM Superpower: Transparency</h2>
      <p>
        If you want to place a spinning 3D logo or a lower-third graphic with a transparent background over a website element, you cannot use a standard <a href="/reference/mp4">MP4</a>. The H.264 standard does not support an Alpha Channel (transparency).
      </p>
      <p>
        WebM (specifically using the VP9 codec) fully supports alpha channels. This makes it the absolute standard format for transparent video on the web.
      </p>

      <h2>Browser Compatibility</h2>
      <p>
        Because it is championed by Google, WebM enjoys perfect support in Chrome, Edge, and Android.
      </p>
      <p>
        Historically, Apple refused to support WebM in Safari, forcing developers to use HEVC with alpha on Apple devices, and WebM everywhere else. However, since macOS Big Sur (Safari 14+), Apple has silently added WebM support, making it nearly universal for modern desktop browsing. (Mobile Safari support remains mixed).
      </p>

      <h2>Convert to WebM</h2>
      <p>
        You can convert your files to WebM format directly in your browser using our <a href="/">local video converter</a>. Because WebM is highly efficient, you can expect excellent <a href="/calculators/video-file-size">file sizes</a>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="bg-surface p-8 rounded-lg border border-border">
      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold mb-4">Format updates</h3>
      <p class="text-secondary mb-4">Get the latest format and compatibility news.</p>
      <NewsletterForm form="newsletter" />
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
